ytopathogenic bacterium Xanthomonas campestris pv. campestris (Xcc) (Brassicaceae black rot), which infects plants of agronomic interest (cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower and the model plant Arabidopsis thaliana).\r\nEarly stages of Xcc entry into plant tissues via hydathodes (leaf margin organs); structure and tissue organization of hydathodes; identification of plant immunity genes controlling infection; molecular dialogue between plant and vascular pathogens during infection.\r\nType 3 effectors (ET3) of Xcc: bacterial proteins injected into plant cells by Type 3 Secretion Systems (SST3) that manipulate host plant physiology (including immunity suppression); search for plant targets of Xcc effectors (by direct or reverse genetic screens and biochemistry).\r\nPhysiological and regulatory processes enabling Xcc to adapt to the “plant” environment; leaf microbiome and impact of Xcc infection on its composition; plant and bacterial co-transcriptomes during the infectious process; in planta screen to identify genes contributing to bacterial fitness during the Xcc life cycle - mutagenesis strategy using labeled transposons; multigenic families such as TonB-dependent Transporters (TBDTs).\r\nGenomic analysis of bacterial strategies used to live in association with plants.\r\nCombined approaches: molecular biology, genetics, association genetics, biochemistry, transcriptomics, genomics, comparative genomics.	https://www.lipme.fr/six-1	https://www.lipme.fr/en/six-1	\N	\N
